window.FIELD_CHAPTER_PARTS["book-3-full-book"] = window.FIELD_CHAPTER_PARTS["book-3-full-book"] || {};
window.FIELD_CHAPTER_PARTS["book-3-full-book"][23] = "<p><strong>Why it works.</strong> A ledger prevents fixation and makes the reasoning visible and reviewable. <strong>When not to build a ledger.</strong> For a simple case with one obvious cause, say the prediction and the check in a sentence.</p>\n<span id=\"c13-c13-8-deciding-and-communicating-fix-roll-back-monitor\"></span>\n<h4>C13.8 Deciding and communicating: fix, roll back, monitor</h4>\n<p>Finding a cause is not the end. The decision depends on severity, reversibility and the cost of waiting. FW-C13-07 gives a matrix.</p>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th></th>\n<th>Easy to reverse</th>\n<th>Hard to reverse</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>High severity</strong> (revenue, safety, trust)</td>\n<td>Roll back or switch off the change now; investigate after</td>\n<td>Stop further spread (pause a staged rollout), fix forward fast, communicate early</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>Low severity</strong></td>\n<td>Fix forward in the normal cycle, or monitor with a threshold</td>\n<td>Monitor, fix in the next release, set expectations</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p>The options are: <em>roll back</em> (undo the change, C17), <em>fix forward</em> (ship a correction), <em>pause</em> (stop a staged rollout where it is, <a href=\"#gl-staged-rollout\">staged rollout</a>), <em>monitor</em> (watch against a threshold set now) and <em>accept</em> (the change was intended or is not worth acting on). For things that cannot be recalled, such as software already installed on devices or an email already sent, the lever is usually to stop further spread and to correct quickly; this is why risky changes go out in stages.</p>\n<p><strong>Communicating.</strong> Say what you know, what you do not, the size, the decision and when the next update comes. A status message has five lines (FW-C13-07): <em>what</em> (the metric and the size), <em>since when</em>, <em>what we know</em>, <em>what we are doing and who owns it</em>, <em>next update at</em>. In an interview, say who you would tell (the owner of the surface, leadership if the size warrants, support if users are affected) and in what order.</p>\n<p><strong>Preventing.</strong> A diagnosis ends with an improvement to detection: an alert on the metric by segment (for example, sign-ups by browser), a check on the release process, a guardrail metric, a runbook for the first fifteen minutes. Say one.</p>\n<p><strong>Why it works.</strong> The matrix ties the decision to risk and speed, and the message keeps stakeholders from guessing. <strong>When not to over-engineer it.</strong> For a small, reversible change, a sentence in the team channel is enough.</p>\n<span id=\"c13-c13-9-metric-rises-diagnosing-good-news\"></span>\n<h4>C13.9 Metric rises: diagnosing good news</h4>\n<p>A metric that goes up deserves the same investigation, with more suspicion, because good news is easy to accept and costly to be wrong about. FW-C13-08 is the check.</p>\n<p><strong>The same steps, plus three suspects.</strong> Confirm that the rise is real and complete (a double-counted event or a changed definition can inflate a metric). Check abuse: a jump in sign-ups or traffic that does not behave like users (it does not activate, comes from one source) is probably not users (the Wikimedia case in section 10 began as an unexplained rise). Check <strong>pull-forward</strong>: demand or activity brought earlier by a promotion or deadline, which raises the metric now and lowers it later, leaving the total roughly unchanged. Take a baseline of 100 sales a week (invented). A promotion week reaches 140, and the next two weeks fall to 80 and 85. Over three weeks that is 305 against a baseline of 300: a rise of 40% followed by a hole, for a net gain of under 2%.</p>\n<p><strong>Check the counter metrics.</strong> If viewing hours rose, what happened to cancellations? If sign-ups rose, what share activated? A rise that comes with a worse counter metric (Worked example 3) may be a mix shift, with heavy users up and light users down.</p>\n<p><strong>Hold the celebration.</strong> Say you would confirm the rise before reporting it and would watch the following weeks for a reversal.</p>\n<p><strong>Why it works.</strong> Good news that is wrong is as costly as bad news that is wrong, and it is checked less often. <strong>When not to dwell.</strong> If a rise has a clear, verified cause (a launch you expected to move it by about that much), confirm the size and move on.</p>\n<span id=\"c13-c13-10-worked-diagnoses-across-product-types\"></span>\n<h4>C13.10 Worked diagnoses across product types</h4>\n<p>Section 5 gives seven diagnoses in seven settings: a search product (seasonality), a social product (a mix shift in a ratio), a streaming service (a rise with a worse counter metric), a marketplace (a supply-side funnel drop), a payments product (rate versus mix) a business-software product (a changed unit), and a fall in activation found with SQL on the practice data of Appendix B (a mix shift). Section 11 adds ten questions with model answers, including two on Apple devices and services, one each set on a Google, a Meta and a Netflix product, and three SQL questions (IQ-C13-08 to IQ-C13-10).</p>\n<span id=\"c13-c13-11-timed-drills\"></span>\n<h4>C13.11 Timed drills</h4>\n<p>The practice protocol in section 13 sets timed drills: a two-minute step zero, a five-minute taxonomy walk-through, a decomposition by hand, and full 20 to 30 minute diagnoses. The SQL drills (C13.6a) are to count distinct users for a stated definition, write a funnel in one query and reconcile two definitions of one metric, each checked with FW-C21-04 and the two checks added in C13.6a.</p>\n<span id=\"c13-3-frameworks\"></span>\n<h3>3. Frameworks</h3>\n<p>Eight frameworks, set out below. The structure library (FW-C03-06), the root-cause toolkit (FW-C03-08), the cohort reading guide (FW-C05-05), the AARRR funnel (FW-C05-07), the trap detector (FW-C12-07) and the six-question statistics check (FW-C12-01) are reused, not repeated.</p>\n<span id=\"fw-c13-01\"></span>\n<h5>FW-C13-01 · The diagnosis spine · Original</h5>\n<p><strong>Core framework.</strong> Learn this one first.</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Inputs:</strong> a metric that moved and 20 to 30 minutes.</li>\n<li><strong>Outputs:</strong> an answer with seven visible steps: clarify, confirm, locate, hypothesize, test, decide, communicate.</li>\n<li><strong>Assumptions:</strong> the interviewer will supply data on request and may change the facts.</li>\n<li><strong>Tradeoffs:</strong> a fixed order makes the reasoning scorable and can feel scripted; adapt to the first evidence.</li>\n<li><strong>Failure modes:</strong> a brainstorm instead of a sequence; skipping the confirmation; no decision; no communication.</li>\n<li><strong>Worked example:</strong> the diagnoses in section 5 and the model answers in section 11.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Steps: <strong>Clarify → Confirm real → Locate → Hypothesize → Test and rule out → Decide → Communicate and prevent.</strong> The time budget is in section 4.</p>\n<span id=\"fw-c13-02\"></span>\n<h5>FW-C13-02 · The step-zero card · Original</h5>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Inputs:</strong> a reported change in a metric.</li>\n<li><strong>Outputs:</strong> answers to six questions and a verdict: real, not real, or unknown, with the next check.</li>\n<li><strong>Assumptions:</strong> you can ask the interviewer or look at the data.</li>\n<li><strong>Tradeoffs:</strong> two minutes of checking can delay the diagnosis and prevents a wasted investigation.</li>\n<li><strong>Failure modes:</strong> accepting the number; treating yesterday's partial data as final; comparing with yesterday instead of with a baseline.</li>\n<li><strong>Worked example:</strong> the incomplete-data check in EX-C13-01 and the noise-band check in EX-C13-02.</li>\n</ul>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>#</th>\n<th>Question</th>\n<th>What it catches</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>1</td>\n<td>What exactly is the metric, per what unit and window, and did the definition change?</td>\n<td>Definition and unit changes</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>2</td>\n<td>How big is the change, absolute and relative?</td>\n<td>Trivial or huge changes</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>3</td>\n<td>What is its shape: step, slide, spike or oscillation?</td>\n<td>Event versus drift</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>4</td>\n<td>What is the baseline (same weekday, last year) and the normal swing?</td>\n<td>Seasonality and noise</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>5</td>\n<td>Is the data complete and settled?</td>\n<td>Lag and partial days</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>6</td>\n<td>Does a second source agree?</td>\n<td>Measurement errors</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<span id=\"fw-c13-03\"></span>\n<h5>FW-C13-03 · The cause taxonomy · Original</h5>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Inputs:</strong> a confirmed change.</li>\n<li><strong>Outputs:</strong> a ranked list of cause families to check in order, each with a tell, a cheap check and an owner.</li>\n<li><strong>Assumptions:</strong> the families are <a href=\"#gl-mece\">MECE</a> enough for the case; a change can have several causes.</li>\n<li><strong>Tradeoffs:</strong> a taxonomy ensures coverage and can feel mechanical; skip families the evidence has already ruled out.</li>\n<li><strong>Failure modes:</strong> starting with the most interesting cause; naming only one family; never reaching &quot;real behavior&quot; by elimination.</li>\n<li><strong>Worked example:</strong> the walk-through in Worked example 1 and the model answers in section 11.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Order: <strong>(1) data and instrumentation, (2) product change, (3) external, (4) mix shift, (5) abuse, (6) a real change in behavior.</strong> The table is in C13.4.</p>\n<span id=\"fw-c13-04\"></span>\n<h5>FW-C13-04 · The segmentation sweep · Original</h5>\n<p><strong>Core framework.</strong> Learn this one first.</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Inputs:</strong> a confirmed change and the available dimensions.</li>\n<li><strong>Outputs:</strong> a table of cuts in order with each segment's absolute contribution to the change and its relative change, and, for a rate, the rate and mix effects.</li>\n<li><strong>Assumptions:</strong> the data can be cut; segments are large enough to be read.</li>\n<li><strong>Tradeoffs:</strong> cuts locate causes and create noise if overdone.</li>\n<li><strong>Failure modes:</strong> unordered slicing; reading only relative changes; ignoring mix.</li>\n<li><strong>Worked example:</strong> the sign-up drop in CS-C13-01 and the payment success rate in Worked example 5.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Cuts in order: time, platform and version, geography, channel, user type, plan, surface, funnel step. For each segment: change in absolute terms, share of the total change, relative change. For a rate: <strong>rate effect = Σ old share × (new rate − old rate); mix effect = Σ (new share − old share) × new rate.</strong></p>\n<span id=\"fw-c13-05\"></span>\n<h5>FW-C13-05 · The funnel and cohort decomposition · Original</h5>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Inputs:</strong> a metric that ends a journey, with step counts before and after.</li>\n<li><strong>Outputs:</strong> step conversion rates before and after, the step with the largest relative fall, the users lost at each step, and a cohort comparison.</li>\n<li><strong>Assumptions:</strong> steps are defined consistently over the period.</li>\n<li><strong>Tradeoffs:</strong> a funnel finds where, and needs other evidence for why.</li>\n<li><strong>Failure modes:</strong> comparing counts instead of rates; ignoring users who moved to an unmeasured path; mixing cohorts of different ages.</li>\n<li><strong>Worked example:</strong> the email-verification step in EX-C13-05 and the provider-response step in Worked example 4.</li>\n</ul>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Step</th>\n<th>Before: count</th>\n<th>Before: rate</th>\n<th>After: count</th>\n<th>After: rate</th>\n<th>Change</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n</table>\n<span id=\"fw-c13-06\"></span>\n<h5>FW-C13-06 · The hypothesis ledger · Original</h5>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Inputs:</strong> a located change and a list of candidate causes.</li>\n<li><strong>Outputs:</strong> for each hypothesis, the other things that would be true if it were the cause, the cheapest check, the result and a status (open, partial, confirmed, ruled out), and an apportionment of the change.</li>\n<li><strong>Assumptions:</strong> the checks can be run in the time available.</li>\n<li><strong>Tradeoffs:</strong> the ledger slows the first minutes and prevents fixation.</li>\n<li><strong>Failure modes:</strong> hypotheses that predict nothing; checks that cannot refute; dropping the second-best too early.</li>\n<li><strong>Worked example:</strong> the browser hypothesis in CS-C13-01, and the ledger for the app-store downloads in IQ-C13-03.</li>\n</ul>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Hypothesis</th>\n<th>If true, we would also see</th>\n<th>Cheapest check</th>\n<th>Result</th>\n<th>Status</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n</table>\n<p>Test each against timing, location and size.</p>\n<span id=\"fw-c13-07\"></span>\n<h5>FW-C13-07 · The decide-and-communicate template · Original</h5>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Inputs:</strong> a diagnosed change, its severity, how reversible the remedy is, and the cost of waiting.</li>\n<li><strong>Outputs:</strong> a decision (roll back, fix forward, pause, monitor, accept), a five-line status message, a prevention step.</li>\n<li><strong>Assumptions:</strong> an owner exists for the surface and the decision.</li>\n<li><strong>Tradeoffs:</strong> speed of action against certainty about the cause.</li>\n<li><strong>Failure modes:</strong> waiting for certainty while harm continues; rolling back without knowing whether the change caused the fall; a message with no next-update time.</li>\n<li><strong>Worked example:</strong> the rollback in CS-C13-01, the supplier-side decision in Worked example 4, and the update-battery decision in IQ-C13-05.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Status message: <strong>What</strong> (the metric and the size); <strong>Since when</strong>; <strong>What we know and don't</strong>; <strong>What we're doing and who owns it</strong>; <strong>Next update at [time].</strong></p>\n<span id=\"fw-c13-08\"></span>\n<h5>FW-C13-08 · The good-news check · Original</h5>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Inputs:</strong> a metric that rose.</li>\n<li><strong>Outputs:</strong> the verdict (real, partly real, not real) after checks for a data error, abuse, pull-forward, mix and counter metrics.</li>\n<li><strong>Assumptions:</strong> counter metrics and post-arrival behavior are available.</li>\n<li><strong>Tradeoffs:</strong> skepticism delays celebration and protects credibility.</li>\n<li><strong>Failure modes:</strong> announcing before checking; treating a spike as a trend; ignoring the counter metric.</li>\n<li><strong>Worked example:</strong> the viewing-hours rise in Worked example 3 and the sign-up jump in IQ-C13-07.</li>\n</ul>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Check</th>\n<th>Question</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>Data</td>\n<td>Is the rise in the measurement (double counting, a definition change)?</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Abuse</td>\n<td>Do the new users behave like users (activate, return) or come from one source?</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Pull-forward</td>\n<td>Does the net over a longer window hold up?</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Mix</td>\n<td>Did the rise come from one segment while another fell?</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Counter metric</td>\n<td>What happened to the metric that should move the other way?</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<span id=\"c13-4-answer-architecture\"></span>\n<h3>4. Answer architecture</h3>\n<span id=\"c13-the-diagnosis-answer-for-a-30-minute-round\"></span>\n<h4>The diagnosis answer, for a 30-minute round</h4>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Step</th>\n<th>Minutes</th>\n<th>What to produce</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>Clarify and confirm</td>\n<td>4</td>\n<td>The step-zero card: metric, size, shape, baseline, completeness; a verdict on whether the change is real</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Locate</td>\n<td>6</td>\n<td>Segment and funnel cuts in order; the share of the change each explains; the locus</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Hypothesize</td>\n<td>5</td>\n<td>The taxonomy in order; two or three candidate causes with what each predicts</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Test and rule out</td>\n<td>8</td>\n<td>The ledger: cheapest check first; timing, location and size tests; an apportionment if mixed</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Decide</td>\n<td>4</td>\n<td>Fix, roll back, pause, monitor or accept, with severity and reversibility; the threshold</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Communicate and prevent</td>\n<td>3</td>\n<td>The five-line status; one preventive step</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p><strong>Pacing rules.</strong> If the interviewer gives data, use it and say what you would look at next. If not, state what you expect to see for each hypothesis, so the interviewer can tell you the result. If you run behind, keep the confirmation, the locus and the decision, and drop breadth in the taxonomy.</p>\n<p><strong>How to say it.</strong> State the order out loud: &quot;I'll first confirm the change is real, then check for anything we shipped, then locate it by segment.&quot; State predictions, not only methods: &quot;If it's the release, I'd expect the drop to start at that hour and sit in the new app version.&quot;</p>\n<p><strong>How to close.</strong> Two sentences: &quot;The evidence points to [cause], which matches the timing, the segment and the size; I'd [decision], tell [stakeholders] now, and add [prevention]. I'd change my view if [observation].&quot;</p>\n<span id=\"c13-type-the-metric-dropped\"></span>\n<h4>Type: the metric dropped</h4>\n<p>Use the full spine. Spend the first third on confirming and locating.</p>\n<span id=\"c13-type-the-metric-rose\"></span>\n<h4>Type: the metric rose</h4>\n<p>Use the good-news check (FW-C13-08). Say you would not report it until verified.</p>\n<span id=\"c13-type-is-this-a-problem\"></span>\n<h4>Type: is this a problem?</h4>\n<p>A movement that may be noise. Use the noise band and baseline from C12, say what threshold would make you investigate, and what you would monitor.</p>\n<span id=\"c13-type-diagnose-and-decide\"></span>\n<h4>Type: diagnose and decide</h4>\n<p>When a decision is required under uncertainty (a rollback, a pause), spend more time on severity, reversibility and the cost of waiting, and on what you would do with partial evidence.</p>\n<span id=\"c13-type-two-metrics-disagree\"></span>\n<h4>Type: two metrics disagree</h4>\n<p>State which is more reliable and why, check the definitions, and look for a mix or a blind spot (a surface that one metric counts and the other does not).</p>\n<span id=\"c13-type-the-short-version-10-minutes\"></span>\n<h4>Type: the short version (10 minutes)</h4>\n<p>Confirm, locate, name the most likely cause and its check, decide and say what you would tell people. Protect the decision.</p>\n<span id=\"c13-common-interviewer-interventions-and-what-to-do\"></span>\n<h4>Common interviewer interventions, and what to do</h4>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Interviewer says</th>\n<th>What it means</th>\n<th>What to do</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>&quot;How do you know it's real?&quot;</td>\n<td>You skipped step zero</td>\n<td>Check the definition, the baseline, completeness and a second source</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>&quot;What would you look at first?&quot;</td>\n<td>They want an order</td>\n<td>State the order and the reason (cheapest and most common first)</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>&quot;What would you expect to see if that's the cause?&quot;</td>\n<td>Your hypothesis predicts nothing</td>\n<td>State timing, location and size predictions</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>&quot;The drop is spread evenly across segments.&quot;</td>\n<td>Not a segment effect</td>\n<td>Think measurement, a global release or an external event</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>&quot;That explains half of it.&quot;</td>\n<td>Mixed causes</td>\n<td>Apportion the change and continue with the remainder</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>&quot;Do you roll back?&quot;</td>\n<td>They want a decision</td>\n<td>Use severity, reversibility and the cost of waiting</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>&quot;What do you tell the CEO?&quot;</td>\n<td>They want the communication</td>\n<td>Give the five-line status, with what you do not know</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<span id=\"c13-5-worked-examples\"></span>\n<h3>5. Worked examples</h3>\n<p>All seven examples are <code>Illustrative</code>. Where a real company's product type is the setting (examples 1 to 3), nothing here is a claim about that company's metrics, systems or decisions, and every figure is an invented assumption. The arithmetic was computed. Each follows the spine in FW-C13-01 and is written as notes you could say aloud; a spoken answer is shorter.</p>\n<span id=\"c13-worked-example-1-search-queries-fell-4-in-one-country-illust\"></span>\n<h4>Worked example 1 — Search: queries fell 4% in one country (Illustrative)</h4>\n<p><em>Setting: a search product of the kind Google operates. No fact about any real product is claimed.</em></p>\n<p><strong>The situation.</strong> Weekly search queries in one country fell 4% week over week (invented): an index of 100 became 96.</p>\n<p><strong>Step zero (FW-C13-02).</strong> The metric is queries, per week. The week is complete. A second source, ad impressions, fell by about the same share (invented). The shape is a one-week dip. The normal week-to-week swing is about ±0.8 on the index (invented), so −4 is five times the usual variation: real, not noise.</p>\n<p><strong>Locate (FW-C13-04).</strong> By device: desktop fell from 30 to 26.4, which is −12%; mobile fell from 70 to 69.6, which is −0.6%. Desktop explains 3.6 of the 4.0 points, 90%. By day: weekdays fell, the weekend did not. By region: the fall is nationwide.</p>\n<p><strong>Hypotheses in order (FW-C13-03).</strong> Data: no change in logging or pipeline, and a second source agrees, so ruled out. Product change: no release touched desktop search that week. External: a weekday-only, desktop-heavy dip across a whole country is what a public-holiday week looks like, when offices and schools close. If true, last year's holiday week should show the same pattern. It did: desktop −11% and mobile −0.5%, which on the index is 30 × (−0.11) + 70 × (−0.005) = −3.3 − 0.35 = −3.65.</p>\n<p><strong>Timing, location, size.</strong> Timing: the dip began on the first holiday and ended with it. Location: desktop and weekdays, as expected. Size: expected −3.65 against observed −4.0; the unexplained 0.35 is well inside the normal ±0.8.</p>\n<p><strong>Decision.</strong> No action. I'd annotate the dashboard, tell the team the dip is a holiday effect of the expected size, and check next week for the rebound (if queries do not recover, the diagnosis is wrong and I'd reopen it). Prevention: add a holiday-adjusted baseline to the weekly view so the same alarm does not recur.</p>\n<p><strong>What this shows.</strong> Most of the work was confirming and comparing with the right baseline. One external cause explained timing, location and size, and the answer was to do nothing.</p>\n<span id=\"c13-worked-example-2-social-comments-per-post-fell-12-illustrati\"></span>\n<h4>Worked example 2 — Social: comments per post fell 12% (Illustrative)</h4>\n<p><em>Setting: a social product of the kind Meta operates. No fact about any real product is claimed.</em></p>\n<p><strong>The situation.</strong> Comments per post fell from 5.0 to 4.4, which is −12% (invented), in the week a new lightweight post type launched. The team asks if the new post type is hurting conversation.</p>\n<p><strong>Step zero.</strong> A ratio has two parts; look at each. Posts per day rose from 1,000,000 to 1,150,000 (+15%). Comments per day rose from 5,000,000 to 5,060,000 (+1.2%). The ratio fell because the denominator grew much faster than the numerator.</p>\n<p><strong>Locate by post type.</strong> Standard posts: 1,000,000 a day, 5.0 comments each, unchanged. New quick posts: 150,000 a day at 0.4 comments each. Total comments: 5,000,000 + 60,000 = 5,060,000. Check the ratio: 5,060,000 ÷ 1,150,000 = 4.4.</p>\n<p><strong>Rate and mix.</strong> The rate within standard posts did not change, so the rate effect is zero. The overall fall is a mix effect: the share of low-comment posts went from 0% to 13% of all posts, and the average fell by (0.13 × 0.4) + (0.87 × 5.0) − 5.0 = −0.6, which is the 5.0 to 4.4 move. This is a mix shift.</p>\n<p><strong>What it does and does not show.</strong> It does not show that conversation got worse: standard posts get exactly as many comments as before, and total comments rose. It does not yet show that the quick post is good. Further checks: whether quick posts replace standard posts for some people (look at users who used both: if their standard posts fell, there is cannibalization, <a href=\"#gl-cannibalization\">cannibalization</a>); comments per user, which does not depend on the number of posts; and whether people who see many quick posts comment less on everything.</p>\n<p><strong>Decision.</strong> Change the report, not the product: track comments per active user and comments per standard post, and report the new post type separately. I'd leave the new post type running with a counter metric for standard-post volume per creator. I'd tell the team that the metric flagged a change in the mix of content, not a loss of conversation. Prevention: define ratio metrics so that a new type of the denominator does not move them silently, and review metric definitions at launch.</p>\n<p><strong>What this shows.</strong> A ratio can fall with both parts rising. Splitting into numerator, denominator and type found a zero rate effect and a pure mix effect.</p>\n<span id=\"c13-worked-example-3-streaming-viewing-hours-rose-while-cancella\"></span>\n<h4>Worked example 3 — Streaming: viewing hours rose while cancellations rose (Illustrative)</h4>\n<p><em>Setting: a streaming service of the kind Netflix operates. No fact about any real product is claimed.</em></p>\n<p><strong>The situation.</strong> After a change to how titles are recommended, average viewing hours per member per month rose 8% (invented). In the same month cancellations rose from 2.04% to 2.39% of members, a 17% relative increase. The team is split between celebrating and worrying.</p>\n<p><strong>Step zero.</strong> Both numbers are complete and defined as before. Two metrics moving in opposite directions is the signal to look for a mix: an average can rise while a large group declines.</p>\n<p><strong>Segment by engagement (FW-C13-04).</strong> Take heavy viewers, 30% of members, and light viewers, 70% of members.</p>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Segment</th>\n<th>Share</th>\n<th>Hours before</th>\n<th>Hours after</th>\n<th>Change</th>\n<th>Cancel before</th>\n<th>Cancel after</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>Heavy viewers</td>\n<td>30%</td>\n<td>20.0</td>\n<td>23.4</td>\n<td>+17%</td>\n<td>0.5%</td>\n<td>0.5%</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Light viewers</td>\n<td>70%</td>\n<td>4.0</td>\n<td>3.55</td>\n<td>−11%</td>\n<td>2.7%</td>\n<td>3.2%</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>All members</td>\n<td>100%</td>\n<td>8.8</td>\n<td>9.5</td>\n<td>+8%</td>\n<td>2.04%</td>\n<td>2.39%</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p>The average rose because heavy viewers watched more (0.3 × 3.4 = +1.02 hours) by more than light viewers watched less (0.7 × −0.45 = −0.32 hours), for a net +0.70 hours, +8%. Cancellations rose entirely among light viewers: 0.7 × (3.2 − 2.7) = +0.35 points of the total +0.35.</p>\n<p><strong>Interpretation.</strong> A hypothesis consistent with all three tests (timing: the same month as the change; location: light viewers; size: the cancellation increase is accounted for by their rate): the change tuned recommendations toward what heavy viewers already like, and light viewers, who need help finding something to watch, found less. Light viewers are the group most likely to cancel. I'd call this a hypothesis, since the data shows who changed, not why.</p>\n<p><strong>Good-news check (FW-C13-08).</strong> The rise in hours is real, and it is concentrated in people who were not at risk. The counter metric, cancellations, moved the wrong way. So I would not report a win.</p>\n<p><strong>Decision.</strong> Run the change as a controlled test if it was not already one (C14), with cancellations among light viewers as a guardrail. Meanwhile limit its exposure: roll it out to heavy viewers and hold it back for light ones, or revert for light viewers, depending on how reversible the change is. Report the two segments separately. Prevention: make cancellation and engagement of light viewers a standing guardrail on recommendation changes.</p>\n<p><strong>What this shows.</strong> An average rose and a counter metric worsened at the same time; the segment split revealed that they were two different groups.</p>\n<span id=\"c13-worked-example-4-marketplace-bookings-fell-9-illustrative\"></span>\n<h4>Worked example 4 — Marketplace: bookings fell 9% (Illustrative)</h4>\n<p><strong>The situation.</strong> Weekly bookings in a home-services marketplace fell from 100,000 to 91,000 (invented), −9%, the week after the company raised the commission it takes from providers from 15% to 20%.</p>\n<p><strong>Step zero.</strong> The week is complete, the definition is unchanged, and the fall is far outside the normal swing of about ±2% (invented). A marketplace has two sides; the pricing change touched providers.</p>\n<p><strong>Funnel (FW-C13-05).</strong></p>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Step</th>\n<th>Before: count</th>\n<th>Before: rate</th>\n<th>After: count</th>\n<th>After: rate</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>Searches</td>\n<td>1,000,000</td>\n<td></td>\n<td>1,000,000</td>\n<td></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Requests sent</td>\n<td>200,000</td>\n<td>20.0% of searches</td>\n<td>198,000</td>\n<td>19.8%</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Provider responds within 24 hours</td>\n<td>140,000</td>\n<td>70.0% of requests</td>\n<td>112,000</td>\n<td>56.6%</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Booked</td>\n<td>100,000</td>\n<td>71.4% of responses</td>\n<td>91,000</td>\n<td>81.3%</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p>Demand is intact: searches are flat and requests fell by only 1%. The break is at provider responses, down 20% in count and from 70% to 56.6% of requests. Customers who got a response booked at a higher rate (71.4% to 81.3%), probably because the providers who still responded were the keener ones.</p>\n<p><strong>Segment the supply side.</strong> Among experienced providers, the share responding to requests fell from 72% to 55% (invented); among providers who joined in the last 60 days it was unchanged. The timing matches the pricing change: the response rate fell from the day the new commission started. Location: experienced providers, who have more alternatives and more to lose. Size: 17 points of response among the larger group accounts for the fall in responses.</p>\n<p><strong>Hypothesis check.</strong> Product change (the commission) fits timing, location and size. Other families: no logging change, no release on the customer side, no external event matched, and no mix shift (the demand mix is stable).</p>\n<p><strong>The decision is not obviously a rollback.</strong> Revenue: before, 100,000 bookings × $80 × 15% = $1,200,000 commission; after, 91,000 × $80 × 20% = $1,456,000, which is 21% higher. The company earns more from fewer bookings. The risk is second-order (<a href=\"#gl-second-order-effect\">second-order effect</a>): providers who stop responding may leave, customers wait longer, and the loss compounds. So the choice is between three options: roll back the commission, keep it, or change its design (for example, a lower rate for high-volume providers, or one that rises with the booking value).</p>\n<p><strong>What I'd do.</strong> Not roll back immediately: the revenue is up, and the change may be intended. But I'd stop treating it as settled: pause any further price changes, track the response rate and the number of active experienced providers weekly, and test a tiered commission on a share of providers (C14). I'd set a threshold in advance: if the response rate has not recovered, or if the active experienced providers fall by more than a set share (say 5%, invented), reduce the rate for them. I'd tell leadership: &quot;Bookings are down 9% because experienced providers respond less since the commission rise; commission revenue is up 21%; the risk is supply attrition, and we're testing a tier.&quot;</p>\n<p><strong>What this shows.</strong> The funnel placed the break at one step on one side of the market; the decision depended on revenue, reversibility and a second-order effect, and not only on the cause.</p>\n<span id=\"c13-worked-example-5-payments-the-success-rate-fell-from-96-0-to\"></span>\n<h4>Worked example 5 — Payments: the success rate fell from 96.0% to 93.4% (Illustrative)</h4>\n<p><strong>The situation.</strong> A payments product's success rate (the share of payment attempts that succeed) fell from 96.0% to 93.4% over two days (invented). The company processes cards and wallets. A new wallet integration launched last week.</p>\n<p><strong>Step zero.</strong> The definition is unchanged. The drop is a step, not a slide. The data is complete. The normal swing is about ±0.3 points, so −2.6 is far outside it.</p>\n<p><strong>Rate effect and mix effect (FW-C13-04).</strong></p>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Method</th>\n<th>Share before</th>\n<th>Success before</th>\n<th>Share after</th>\n<th>Success after</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>Cards</td>\n<td>80%</td>\n<td>97%</td>\n<td>70%</td>\n<td>94%</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Wallets</td>\n<td>20%</td>\n<td>92%</td>\n<td>30%</td>\n<td>92%</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Overall</td>\n<td></td>\n<td>96.0%</td>\n<td></td>\n<td>93.4%</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p>The split (the calculation is in C13.5): rate effect −2.4, mix effect −0.2, total −2.6. So 92% of the fall (2.4 of 2.6) is a drop in the card success rate. The shift toward wallets, which succeed less often than cards, explains only 0.2 points.</p>\n<p><strong>Locate within cards.</strong> By issuing bank: one issuer accounts for 30% of card volume. If the other issuers are unchanged at 97%, then for the card rate to be 94%, this issuer's success rate is (94 − 0.7 × 97) ÷ 0.3 = 87%. Its success fell from 97% to 87%, ten points.</p>\n<p><strong>Causes.</strong> Data: a second source (the payment provider's own report) shows the same failures, so it is real. Product change: no release in the card path. External: a failure concentrated in one issuer with a matching error code (timeouts) points to that issuer's systems or the route to them. Abuse: no change in fraud-block rates.</p>\n<p><strong>Decision.</strong> The severity is high (payments are revenue and trust) and the remedy is reversible: route that issuer's transactions through a backup acquirer or retry on failure, if available, and contact the provider. Say to merchants: &quot;A subset of card payments from one issuer are failing; we have rerouted; the success rate is recovering; next update in an hour.&quot; Prevention: alert on success rate by issuer, not only overall, since the overall rate hid a ten-point failure in a third of card volume.</p>\n<p><strong>What this shows.</strong> The decomposition showed at once that the fall was within cards and not the mix, and the nested cut found the issuer.</p>\n<span id=\"c13-worked-example-6-business-software-weekly-active-accounts-fe\"></span>\n<h4>Worked example 6 — Business software: weekly active accounts fell 6% (Illustrative)</h4>\n<p><strong>The situation.</strong> A business-software product reported weekly active accounts falling from 5,000 to 4,700, −6% (invented), in one week. A renewal season is not due.</p>\n<p><strong>Step zero.</strong> The definition is unchanged on paper, but the unit is accounts. A data-cleanup project ran last week. Weekly active users, the same product measured per person, went from 41,000 to 41,100, up 0.2%. Accounts fell and people did not: the unit is the suspect.</p>\n<p><strong>Locate.</strong> The cleanup merged 480 duplicate accounts (created when customers signed up twice) into 240. Each merged account counts once. That removed 240 accounts from the count, with no change in how many people use the product. The remaining change: 300 − 240 = 60 accounts, or −1.2% (the unexplained part), within the normal week-to-week swing of about ±1.5% (invented).</p>\n<p><strong>Taxonomy.</strong> Data and instrumentation (family 1): a unit change from a merge. Product, external, mix, abuse: nothing indicates otherwise. Real behavior: people are flat.</p>\n<p><strong>Decision.</strong> There is no problem with customers. Restate the history on the new definition (backfill) so the trend is comparable, annotate the dashboard, and report both weekly active accounts and weekly active users, with a note on which answers which question: accounts for customer counts, users for adoption. Tell the sales and customer success teams who track account counts. Prevention: when a data project can change a metric's unit or count, announce it and plan the restatement beforehand.</p>\n<p><strong>What this shows.</strong> A 6% &quot;drop&quot; was a change of unit. Checking a second metric in two minutes avoided an investigation.</p>\n<span id=\"c13-worked-example-7-activation-fell-6-5-points-in-three-weeks-f\"></span>\n<h4>Worked example 7 — Activation fell 6.5 points in three weeks: finding the mix shift with SQL (Illustrative)</h4>\n<p><em>The data are synthetic and invented (invented); the outputs below were produced by running the queries on them.</em></p>\n<p><strong>The question.</strong> Activation, the share of new users who activate, fell from about 54% to 47.5% across three weekly signup cohorts. What happened?</p>\n<p><strong>The data.</strong> A table <code>users(user_id, signup_date, channel, activated)</code> with three weekly cohorts of 2,000 signups each (Appendix B, script 2, seed 11). By construction, each channel has the same activation probability every week and only the channel mix changes, so the right answer is known.</p>\n<p><strong>Step zero (C13.3).</strong> Each cohort has 2,000 signups, so there is no data gap; the definition of <code>activated</code> is one column and has not changed.</p>\n<p><strong>Query 1: the metric by week.</strong></p>\n<pre><code class=\"language-sql\">SELECT CAST((julianday(signup_date) - julianday('2027-03-01')) / 7 AS INT) + 1 AS wk,\n       COUNT(*) AS signups, SUM(activated) AS activated,\n       ROUND(100.0 * SUM(activated) / COUNT(*), 2) AS activation_pct\nFROM users GROUP BY wk ORDER BY wk;\n</code></pre>\n<p>Output: week 1, 2,000 signups, 1,079 activated, 53.95%; week 2, 2,000, 1,022, 51.10%; week 3, 2,000, 950, 47.50%. The fall is real: a 6.45-point drop.</p>\n<p><strong>Query 2: share of signups and activation rate, by week and channel.</strong></p>\n<pre><code class=\"language-sql\">WITH t AS (SELECT CAST((julianday(signup_date) - julianday('2027-03-01')) / 7 AS INT) + 1 AS wk,\n                  channel, COUNT(*) AS n, SUM(activated) AS a\n           FROM users GROUP BY wk, channel),\n     w AS (SELECT wk, SUM(n) AS total FROM t GROUP BY wk)\nSELECT t.wk, t.channel, t.n, ROUND(100.0 * t.n / w.total, 1) AS share_pct,\n       ROUND(100.0 * t.a / t.n, 1) AS rate_pct\nFROM t JOIN w USING (wk) ORDER BY t.wk, t.channel;\n</code></pre>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Week</th>\n<th>Channel</th>\n<th>Signups</th>\n<th>Share of signups</th>\n<th>Activation rate</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>1</td>\n<td>organic</td>\n<td>1,057</td>\n<td>52.9%</td>\n<td>53.4%</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>1</td>\n<td>paid</td>\n<td>400</td>\n<td>20.0%</td>\n<td>38.0%</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>1</td>\n<td>referral</td>\n<td>543</td>\n<td>27.1%</td>\n<td>66.9%</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>2</td>\n<td>organic</td>\n<td>890</td>\n<td>44.5%</td>\n<td>52.2%</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>2</td>\n<td>paid</td>\n<td>698</td>\n<td>34.9%</td>\n<td>41.3%</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>2</td>\n<td>referral</td>\n<td>412</td>\n<td>20.6%</td>\n<td>65.3%</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>3</td>\n<td>organic</td>\n<td>707</td>\n<td>35.4%</td>\n<td>52.1%</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>3</td>\n<td>paid</td>\n<td>986</td>\n<td>49.3%</td>\n<td>39.4%</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>3</td>\n<td>referral</td>\n<td>307</td>\n<td>15.3%</td>\n<td>63.2%</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p><strong>Read it, and check it by hand.</strong> Paid's share rose from 20.0% to 49.3% while organic and referral shrank; the rate inside each channel barely moved (organic 53.4% to 52.1%, paid 38.0% to 39.4%, referral 66.9% to 63.2%). Check the overall rate from the table (FW-C13-04, overall = Σ share × rate): week 1 is 0.529 × 53.4 + 0.200 × 38.0 + 0.2715 × 66.9, about 54.0; week 3 is 0.3535 × 52.1 + 0.493 × 39.4 + 0.1535 × 63.2, about 47.5. Both match Query 1.</p>\n<p><strong>Query 3: split the change into a rate effect and a mix effect</strong> (the convention of FW-C13-04: rate effect at the old shares, mix effect at the new rates).</p>\n<pre><code class=\"language-sql\">WITH t AS (SELECT CAST((julianday(signup_date) - julianday('2027-03-01')) / 7 AS INT) + 1 AS wk,\n                  channel, COUNT(*) AS n, SUM(activated) AS a\n           FROM users GROUP BY wk, channel),\n     w AS (SELECT wk, SUM(n) AS total FROM t GROUP BY wk),\n     s AS (SELECT t.wk, t.channel, 1.0 * t.n / w.total AS share, 1.0 * t.a / t.n AS rate\n           FROM t JOIN w USING (wk)),\n     x AS (SELECT s1.channel, s1.share AS sh1, s1.rate AS r1, s3.share AS sh3, s3.rate AS r3\n           FROM s s1 JOIN s s3 ON s1.channel = s3.channel AND s1.wk = 1 AND s3.wk = 3)\nSELECT ROUND(100 * SUM(sh1 * (r3 - r1)), 2)  AS rate_effect_pp,\n       ROUND(100 * SUM((sh3 - sh1) * r3), 2) AS mix_effect_pp,\n       ROUND(100 * SUM(sh3 * r3) - 100 * SUM(sh1 * r1), 2) AS total_change_pp\nFROM x;\n</code></pre>\n<p>Output: rate effect −1.41 points, mix effect −5.04 points, total −6.45 points; the two effects add to the total. Putting the week-3 channel rates at the week-1 mix gives 52.54%, against 53.95% in week 1 and 47.50% in week 3.</p>\n<p><strong>Interpret.</strong> 5.04 of the 6.45 points, about 78%, are mix: more users from the paid channel, where activation is lower. The −1.41 rate effect comes from within-channel differences of one to four points on 300 to 1,000 users per channel-week, which are inside sampling noise (C13.3), and in this dataset the generator holds each rate fixed. The right reading is &quot;no evidence of a change within any channel.&quot;</p>\n<p><strong>What to do.</strong> This is not a product defect, so do not roll back. Ask whether the lower activation of paid users is acceptable given what they cost and what they are worth (C20.7); look at onboarding for the paid channel; report a mix-adjusted activation figure beside the headline so that the average does not mislead; and add channel mix to the dashboard as a guardrail (C13.8).</p>\n<p><strong>What this shows.</strong> Three queries turned &quot;activation fell&quot; into a located cause with numbers that could be checked by hand and a rule for reporting: the headline can fall with no change in how any channel behaves.</p>\n<span id=\"c13-6-baseline-strong-standout\"></span>\n<h3>6. Baseline, Strong, Standout</h3>\n<p><strong>Prompt:</strong> &quot;Daily active users dropped 5% yesterday. How do you investigate?&quot;</p>\n<span id=\"c13-baseline-answer\"></span>\n<h4>Baseline answer</h4>\n<p>&quot;I'd look at the data to see what's going on. It could be a bug, a competitor, a holiday or seasonality. I'd talk to engineering and marketing, and fix whatever we find.&quot;</p>\n<p><em>What is wrong:</em> a list of causes with no order, no tests and no definition of &quot;real.&quot; Nothing says what would distinguish one cause from another, and there is no decision or communication.</p>\n<span id=\"c13-strong-answer\"></span>\n<h4>Strong answer</h4>\n<p>&quot;First I'd confirm the drop is real: what the metric is, whether yesterday's data is complete, how it compares with the same weekday last week and with the normal range, and whether another source agrees. If daily swings are usually around 2%, then 5% is outside the noise.</p>\n<p>&quot;Then I'd locate it. I'd cut by platform and app version, country, new versus returning users and acquisition channel, and see where most of the drop sits. A drop concentrated in one platform and version suggests a release; a drop across everything suggests measurement or something external.</p>\n<p>&quot;I'd work through causes in order: data and logging, anything we shipped, external events like holidays or outages, a change in the mix of users, and abuse or bot cleanup. For each, I'd say what I'd expect: if it's a release, the drop should start at the release time and sit in the new version. If I find a cause that fits timing, location and size, I'd decide between a rollback and a fix, and I'd tell the owner of the surface and leadership what we know.&quot;</p>\n<span id=\"c13-standout-answer\"></span>\n<h4>Standout answer</h4>\n<p>Everything in the Strong answer, plus:</p>\n<p>&quot;I'd keep a ledger of hypotheses with predictions and the cheapest check for each, so that I rule things out and do not collect stories. I'd quantify as I go: if one platform explains 85% of the drop I'd go deep there, and if the cuts show an even spread I'd suspect a measurement or global cause.</p>\n<p>&quot;For a rate or an average I'd split the change into a rate effect and a mix effect, since a mix shift can produce a drop with no change in behavior. I'd also look at where missing users went: a metric can fall because people moved to a surface it does not count.</p>\n<p>&quot;On the decision: it depends on severity and reversibility. If the cause is a release and the drop is large, I'd roll back first and investigate after, since rolling back is cheap. If it's a pricing change that raised revenue, I'd not roll back automatically; I'd weigh revenue against second-order effects and set a threshold. I'd say in advance what would make me act.</p>\n<p>&quot;The message to stakeholders is five lines: what and how big, since when, what we know and don't, who owns the fix, and when the next update comes. And I'd add one preventive step, such as an alert on the metric by platform and version.</p>\n<p>&quot;What would change my mind: if the drop disappears when the latest data finishes arriving, then it was lag and not a decline.&quot;</p>\n<span id=\"c13-what-moves-an-answer-up-a-band\"></span>\n<h4>What moves an answer up a band</h4>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>From Baseline to Strong</th>\n<th>From Strong to Standout</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>Confirms the change and compares with a baseline</td>\n<td>Keeps a ledger with predictions and refuting checks, and quantifies each cut</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Locates by segment before listing causes</td>\n<td>Splits rate and mix, and asks where missing users went</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Orders causes and says what each predicts</td>\n<td>Ties the decision to severity, reversibility and second-order effects</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Names a decision and who to tell</td>\n<td>Gives the five-line message, a prevention step and what would change the view</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<span id=\"c13-7-company-lens\"></span>\n<h3>7. Company lens</h3>\n<p>All claims describe what companies say publicly or what candidates commonly report, not insider knowledge. Interview formats change <code>[as of 2026-10]</code>.</p>\n<blockquote>\n<p><strong>Company lens: Google [as of 2026-10].</strong> Google's published book on site reliability engineering describes blameless postmortems and a structured approach to incidents <code>Public</code> <code>[V: Google SRE book, 2016, and sre.google]</code>. Candidates commonly report analytical questions on a metric movement at large scale <code>Inferred</code>. Practical consequence: state the order of your checks, give the cheapest first, and end with the prevention step; at scale a small percentage is a large number of people.</p>\n</blockquote>\n<blockquote>\n<p><strong>Company lens: Amazon [as of 2026-10].</strong> Amazon's leadership principles include Dive Deep, which asks leaders to probe the data when anecdote and metric disagree <code>Public</code> <code>[V: Amazon's Leadership Principles]</code>, and the company is publicly associated with a written practice of correcting errors after an incident (not rechecked here) <code>Inferred</code>. Candidates commonly report follow-ups on whether a number could be wrong <code>Inferred</code>. Practical consequence: lead with whether the number is real, and describe the correction and the mechanism that prevents recurrence.</p>\n</blockquote>\n<blockquote>\n<p><strong>Company lens: Meta [as of 2026-10].</strong> Meta has published postmortems of large outages <code>Public</code> <code>[V: Meta engineering post of 5 October 2021]</code>, and candidates commonly report metric-drop prompts about engagement, paired with questions on tradeoffs between engagement and wellbeing <code>Inferred</code>. Practical consequence: when a metric falls, ask whether it is a measurement artifact, a mix shift or a deliberate decision, and weigh a fall in engagement against what the change was meant to do.</p>\n</blockquote>\n<blockquote>\n<p><strong>Company lens: Microsoft [as of 2026-10].</strong> Microsoft publishes post-incident reviews for major cloud outages <code>Public</code> <code>[V: Azure status history publishes post incident reviews]</code>, and its enterprise products carry customers who depend on current behavior <code>Inferred</code>. Practical consequence: separate customer-impacting regressions from internal metric noise, and say how you would communicate to customers during the diagnosis.</p>\n</blockquote>\n<blockquote>\n<p><strong>Company lens: Apple [as of 2026-10].</strong> Apple ships software updates to a very large installed base of devices <code>Public</code> <code>[V: Apple Q1 fiscal 2026 results, 29 January 2026: more than 2.5 billion active devices; yearly major software releases in September after June WWDC]</code>, and third-party guides report that diagnosis prompts at Apple concern behavior after an update, where a change is hard to undo once installed <code>Inferred</code>. Practical consequence: weigh the irreversibility of an update, name the lever (pause the rollout, ship a fix, set expectations) and separate transient effects from persistent ones.</p>\n</blockquote>\n<blockquote>\n<p><strong>Company lens: Netflix [as of 2026-10].</strong> Netflix publishes technical posts on experimentation and on resilience testing <code>Public</code> <code>[V: Netflix Tech Blog posts on experimentation and resilience testing]</code>, and candidates commonly report data-informed questions on viewing and retention <code>Inferred</code>. Practical consequence: when engagement rises, look at who it rose for and check a retention counter metric before calling it a win.</p>\n</blockquote>\n<blockquote>\n<p><strong>Company lens: Stripe [as of 2026-10].</strong> Payments metrics such as success rates depend on card networks, issuers and partners, and Stripe is widely reported to publish a public status page for its services, which was not opened here <code>Inferred</code>; candidates report questions on authorization and failure rates <code>Inferred</code>. Practical consequence: break a success rate down by method, issuer and error code before concluding the product is at fault.</p>\n</blockquote>\n<span id=\"c13-8-common-mistakes\"></span>\n<h3>8. Common mistakes</h3>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>#</th>\n<th>Mistake</th>\n<th>How to detect it</th>\n<th>Fix</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>1</td>\n<td>Brainstorming causes with no order</td>\n<td>A list of eight causes in two minutes</td>\n<td>State an order, cheapest and most common first</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>2</td>\n<td>Skipping &quot;is it real?&quot;</td>\n<td>You explain a change before checking the data</td>\n<td>Check definition, baseline, completeness and a second source</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>3</td>\n<td>Comparing with yesterday</td>\n<td>The baseline is one day</td>\n<td>Use the same weekday and the same period last year, and the noise band</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>4</td>\n<td>Trusting partial data</td>\n<td>A morning dashboard shows a sharp fall</td>\n<td>Compare the same hours on earlier days or wait for the data to settle</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>5</td>\n<td>Naming a cause that predicts nothing</td>\n<td>&quot;Probably a competitor&quot;</td>\n<td>State what you would see if it were true; check it</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>6</td>\n<td>Slicing by everything</td>\n<td>Many cuts, one odd segment</td>\n<td>Cut in a planned order; require timing and size to fit</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>7</td>\n<td>Reading only relative changes</td>\n<td>A segment that fell 20% is a tiny share</td>\n<td>Report absolute contributions with relative changes</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>8</td>\n<td>Missing a mix shift</td>\n<td>An average moved with no behavior change</td>\n<td>Split rate and mix effects</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>9</td>\n<td>Stopping at the cause</td>\n<td>No decision or message</td>\n<td>Decide, with severity and reversibility, and write the five lines</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>10</td>\n<td>Celebrating a rise</td>\n<td>You announce good news at once</td>\n<td>Run the good-news check and watch the next weeks</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<span id=\"c13-9-mental-models-applied\"></span>\n<h3>9. Mental models applied</h3>\n<p>Four models, each tied to a decision in this chapter. <a href=\"#gl-pareto-principle\">Pareto principle</a> comes from C03 and <a href=\"#gl-reversible-decision\">reversible decision</a> from C02.</p>\n<p><strong>Boring causes first</strong> (<code>MM-boring-first</code>). When something suddenly changes, the explanation is more often mundane (a logging change, a release, a holiday, a partner outage) than interesting (a shift in user psychology, a competitor's clever move) <code>Inferred</code>. Check the boring causes first because they are cheap to test and common, and because an exciting story is a poor reason to skip them.</p>\n<p><strong>The vital few</strong> (<code>MM-vital-few</code>, see <a href=\"#gl-pareto-principle\">Pareto principle</a>, C03). A few segments usually explain most of a change. Find them with contribution analysis and go deep there; an even spread across segments is itself a clue, pointing at a cause that touches everyone.</p>\n<p><strong>Fixation</strong> (<code>MM-fixation</code>). The first plausible explanation becomes the frame, and later evidence is read through it. A detective who decides early on the butler looks for butler-shaped facts. The defense is a ledger that records predictions in advance, and the habit of looking for the observation that would show your favorite is wrong.</p>\n<p><strong>Blast radius and reversibility</strong> (<code>MM-blast-radius</code>, see <a href=\"#gl-reversible-decision\">reversible decision</a>, C02). How much damage a wrong action does, and how easily it can be undone, should set how fast and how boldly you act. When a change is easy to reverse, act first and investigate after; when it is hard to undo (installed software, an email already sent), contain the spread first. The same logic is why risky changes ship in stages.</p>\n<span id=\"c13-engineering-design-and-data-science-perspectives\"></span>\n<h3>Engineering, design and data-science perspectives</h3>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Engineering.</strong> Engineers know what shipped, when, and to whom. Ask for the release log, the flag changes, the experiments running and any recent change to logging or the pipeline in the first five minutes, and ask whether the change can be switched off. Bring them in as partners at the start; the fastest diagnoses are joint.</li>\n<li><strong>Design and research.</strong> Numbers say where; people say why. Watch recorded sessions or run a few quick interviews with users in the affected segment, read support tickets and app-store reviews from the period, and look at the screen where the funnel breaks. A form that confuses people is visible in minutes of watching.</li>\n<li><strong>Data science.</strong> Ask the data scientist for the baseline, the noise band, the breakdown by the main segments and the definition history. Share your ledger so they can suggest the checks that would refute your leading hypothesis, and ask what the data cannot show. They can also set up the alert that prevents the next surprise.</li>\n<li><strong>Support, operations and trust and safety.</strong> Support sees a problem first, in tickets and chats, often before a dashboard moves; trust and safety knows about abuse and cleanups that change counts. Ask both early: &quot;What are you hearing?&quot; and &quot;Did we change anything about bots or spam?&quot;</li>\n</ul>\n<span id=\"c13-checklist-before-you-declare-a-cause\"></span>\n<h3>Checklist: before you declare a cause</h3>\n<ol>\n<li>Did I confirm the change is real: definition, baseline, completeness, second source?</li>\n<li>Did I check the cheap, common families first: data, then our own changes, then external?</li>\n<li>Did I locate the change with segments and funnel steps, and quantify the share each explains?</li>\n<li>Does the cause match on timing, location and size?</li>\n<li>Did I separate rate and mix effects for any average or rate?</li>\n<li>Did I look for a second cause for the part that is unexplained?</li>\n<li>Did I choose fix, roll back, pause, monitor or accept, with severity and reversibility in view?</li>\n<li>Do I have a five-line message and one preventive step?</li>\n</ol>\n<span id=\"c13-10-case-studies\"></span>\n<h3>10. Case studies</h3>\n<span id=\"cs-c13-01\"></span>\n<h5>CS-C13-01 · The sign-up drop that was one browser · Illustrative · Beginner</h5>\n<p><em>Fictional composite. Every number is invented (invented).</em></p>\n<p><strong>Situation.</strong> A consumer app normally receives about 10,000 sign-ups a day. At 9:00 on a Wednesday the dashboard shows 8,000 for Tuesday, a drop of 20%. A new version of the sign-up form was released on Tuesday at 08:10. The growth lead asks the PM what happened.</p>\n<p><strong>Constraints.</strong> No weekday events, no marketing changes, and a team that can roll back the release in under an hour. Sign-ups are the product's main entry point, so every hour matters.</p>\n<p><strong>Data.</strong> The normal day-to-day swing is about ±600 sign-ups, so −2,000 is far outside it. The data is complete for the day, and the app-store consoles show the same pattern. Sign-ups by platform and, within web, by browser:</p>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Segment</th>\n<th>Normal day</th>\n<th>Tuesday</th>\n<th>Change</th>\n<th>Share of the change</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>iOS app</td>\n<td>3,500</td>\n<td>3,480</td>\n<td>−20</td>\n<td>1%</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Android app</td>\n<td>2,500</td>\n<td>2,490</td>\n<td>−10</td>\n<td>0.5%</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Web, Browser S</td>\n<td>2,400</td>\n<td>440</td>\n<td>−1,960</td>\n<td>98%</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Web, other browsers (Chrome-type)</td>\n<td>1,100</td>\n<td>1,095</td>\n<td>−5</td>\n<td>0.25%</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Web, rest</td>\n<td>500</td>\n<td>495</td>\n<td>−5</td>\n<td>0.25%</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Total</td>\n<td>10,000</td>\n<td>8,000</td>\n<td>−2,000</td>\n<td>100%</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p>By the hour, Browser S sign-ups fell at 08:10 and stayed low; the other segments did not move. The error log for the sign-up call shows a script error only for older versions of Browser S, beginning at 08:10.</p>\n<p><strong>Problem definition.</strong> As an observable problem: &quot;Since the release at 08:10, users on older versions of one browser cannot complete sign-up.&quot;</p>\n<p><strong>Analysis.</strong> Step zero rules out noise, incomplete data and a measurement problem: two sources agree and the size is far outside the swing. The segmentation sweep (FW-C13-04) finds that one browser explains 98% of the change, a clear vital-few pattern (<a href=\"#gl-pareto-principle\">Pareto principle</a>). The ledger (FW-C13-06): <em>a release broke sign-up on that browser</em> predicts that the drop starts at 08:10 (yes), is concentrated in that browser (yes, 98%), and appears in the error logs (yes). The size fits: the browser provided 24% of normal sign-ups, and it lost 82% of its sign-ups for the day, which is 1,960 of 2,400. Competing hypotheses fail: a holiday or competitor would not hit one browser at one minute past a release, and a logging error would not show script errors for users.</p>\n<p><strong>Options.</strong></p>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Option</th>\n<th>Optimizes</th>\n<th>Costs</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>A. Roll back the sign-up form release</td>\n<td>Speed of recovery</td>\n<td>Loses whatever the release added; the cause is already known</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>B. Hotfix the form for the older browsers</td>\n<td>Keeps the release</td>\n<td>Takes longer; risk of another mistake</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>C. Wait and monitor</td>\n<td>Certainty</td>\n<td>Continues the loss: about 2,400 × 0.8 ≈ 1,900 sign-ups a day</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p><strong>Tradeoffs.</strong> A is reversible and fastest. B is better if the fix is small and the release valuable, but it is slower. C costs about 80 sign-ups an hour on average (1,900 a day) and has no value, since the cause is clear.</p>\n<p><strong>Decision.</strong> Roll back now (A), then hotfix and re-release with the browser tested. Message (FW-C13-07): &quot;Sign-ups fell 20% on Tuesday because the form release at 08:10 broke sign-up on older versions of one browser, about 1,960 sign-ups lost. Rolling back now. Owner: web engineering. Next update at 11:00.&quot; Prevention: an alert on sign-up conversion by browser and version, and a browser test matrix in the release checklist.</p>\n<p><strong>Outcome.</strong> In this fictional scenario the rollback finished at 09:40, Browser S sign-ups returned to about 2,350 a day by Thursday, and the total loss was about 2,000 sign-ups from Tuesday plus about 600 from Wednesday morning (invented). The hotfix shipped the following week with the browser added to the test matrix.</p>\n<p><strong>Alternative outcomes.</strong> If the segments had all fallen evenly, the cause would have been measurement or a global event and the team would have checked logging and external factors. If the browser had explained only half the drop, the other half would need its own investigation, and the PM would have apportioned the change. If a rollback had been impossible (a database change shipped with the form), the team would have moved to the hotfix and told users on the affected browser to use another until it landed. The lesson is the order: confirm, locate, test the hypothesis against timing, location and size, then decide.</p>\n<span id=\"cs-c13-02\"></span>\n<h5>CS-C13-02 · Wikimedia, 2025: a traffic spike that was bots, and the decline behind it · Verified · Advanced</h5>\n<p><em>Sources (types): the blog post &quot;New User Trends on Wikipedia&quot; by Marshall Miller of the Wikimedia Foundation, published on the Foundation's Diff blog on 17 October 2025, read in full (primary, the organization's own account); and TechCrunch's report of it (18 October 2025). Wikimedia's attribution of the decline to generative AI features and social video is the organization's own interpretation, reported as such. The analysis is the author's. Re-read in October 2026.</em></p>\n<p><strong>Situation.</strong> The Wikimedia Foundation classifies traffic to Wikipedia as human or bot. Around May 2025 it noticed unusually high amounts of apparently human traffic, mostly from Brazil <code>[V: Foundation post, 17 October 2025]</code>. Its engineers and analysts investigated and found bots built to evade detection. The Foundation updated its bot-detection systems and re-applied the new logic to traffic from March through August 2025. After the revision, the Foundation reported declines in human pageviews &quot;over the past few months&quot; of roughly 8% compared with the same months in 2024, and said it believes the declines reflect the impact of generative AI and social media on how people seek information <code>[V: Foundation post]</code>.</p>\n<p><strong>Constraints.</strong> Classification of traffic depends on detection rules, which change over time, and the Foundation said that revising its data this way means it has to be interpreted with care, because its bot detection applies different rules at different points in time. Bots designed to look human are hard to separate from people by definition. The organization depends on human readers, since fewer visits mean fewer volunteers and donors, as the Foundation's post says. It cannot observe why a person did not visit.</p>\n<p><strong>Data.</strong> As reported: an unexpected rise in apparently human traffic in May and June 2025, concentrated by country; a finding, on inspection, that the traffic did not behave like people; a revised classification applied back to March; a resulting decline in human pageviews of roughly 8% compared with the same months in 2024, described as &quot;over the past few months&quot; (the post does not name the months) <code>[V: Foundation post]</code>. The Foundation also said that in April 2025 it had described how bots and crawlers overburden its infrastructure <code>[V: Foundation post]</code>. Other estimates of the decline from outside sources differ and measure different things, so they are not used here.</p>\n<p><strong>Problem definition.</strong> <em>The author's reading.</em> A number that looked like good news (more human traffic) did not behave like human traffic. The question became twofold: how many people actually read the site, and how has that changed? As an observable problem: &quot;The count of human pageviews was inflated by traffic that was not human, so the headline trend was wrong.&quot;</p>\n<p><strong>Analysis.</strong> <em>The author's analysis.</em> Read through this chapter's frameworks, the sequence is a textbook diagnosis of a rise (FW-C13-08). The team noticed an anomaly and its location (a country) before accepting it, which is the segmentation habit. It checked behavior after arrival, since traffic that does not behave like users is a sign of abuse (family 5). It treated the classification itself as a possible cause, family 1 (instrumentation), by revising the detection rules and re-applying them to past data, which restated the baseline. Only then did a trend appear: a fall of about 8% in human pageviews. So the good news was false, and the underlying trend was a decline.</p>\n<p>The explanation for the decline is a separate step. The Foundation attributes it to generative AI features in search and chatbots and to social platforms, which supply information without sending readers to the site <code>[V: Foundation post]</code>. That is a reasonable hypothesis consistent with the timing and with other publishers' reports, and the data used here do not establish it. The case is useful for a PM because it shows both stages: first make the number trustworthy, then diagnose it, and be explicit about which parts are measured and which are inferred. The caveat the Foundation itself gave, that the revised data must be interpreted with care because detection rules differ over time, is part of a good communication.</p>\n<p><strong>Options.</strong> <em>The author's reconstruction of choices an analytics team could have faced; not a record of the Foundation's deliberations.</em></p>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Option</th>\n<th>Optimizes</th>\n<th>Costs</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>A. Report the high May and June traffic as growth</td>\n<td>A better-looking trend</td>\n<td>A false number; a decision based on it; lost credibility when discovered</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>B. Investigate, revise detection, restate the series and publish with the caveat</td>\n<td>A trustworthy picture and a basis for strategy</td>\n<td>A less welcome message; a break in comparability</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>C. Investigate and keep the finding internal</td>\n<td>Control of the message</td>\n<td>The Foundation's partners and community make decisions with the old figures</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p><strong>Tradeoffs.</strong> B costs a bad headline and a series that cannot be compared exactly with earlier ones, and gives a number that can be acted on. A is the easiest and the most damaging. C avoids embarrassment and leaves outsiders working from the wrong data.</p>\n<p><strong>Decision.</strong> As documented, the Foundation updated its detection, reclassified the past months, and published the finding, with a caution that the revised data must be interpreted with care and a plan that includes enforcing policies on third-party use, developing an attribution framework, and two new readers teams <code>[V: Foundation post]</code>.</p>\n<p><strong>Outcome.</strong> Reported: the 8% decline in human pageviews and the Foundation's work on attribution and reader growth. What the decline will do to editing and donations, and how much of it AI features explain, is not established in the sources used here.</p>\n<p><strong>Alternative outcomes.</strong> <em>Inference.</em> Had the team accepted the spike, the decline would have been hidden for longer and any decision about reader growth would have rested on inflated data. Had the revised detection been too aggressive, real people could have been classified as bots and the decline overstated, so the error can run in both directions; validating the classifier on a known sample is the check. A reader should also see the wider lesson for any PM: when a metric rises for no reason you can name, treat it as a diagnosis, and be ready for the corrected number to be worse than the first.</p>\n<span id=\"c13-11-interview-questions-and-model-answers\"></span>\n<h3>11. Interview questions and model answers</h3>\n<p>Ten questions: three Beginner, three Intermediate, four Hard. Model answers are <code>Illustrative</code>; every figure is an assumption, and the arithmetic was computed. Each is written against the six behaviors in section 1 and uses the spine in FW-C13-01. Where a real company's product is the setting, the answer reasons from public information and user behavior and states no internal fact. The answers are fuller than a first pass in a real round; a spoken version keeps the same order with fewer words.</p>\n<span id=\"iq-c13-01\"></span>\n<h5>IQ-C13-01 · Beginner · analytics · AN</h5>\n<p><strong>Prompt.</strong> Weekly sign-ups fell 3% this week. Is it a problem?</p>\n<p><strong>Rubric.</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Baseline:</strong> &quot;Yes, any drop is a problem, so I'd investigate,&quot; or &quot;no, 3% is small.&quot;</li>\n<li><strong>Strong:</strong> Compares the change with the normal variation and a baseline before deciding, and says what would trigger a deeper look.</li>\n<li><strong>Standout:</strong> Computes a noise band from history, notes the limits of a short history and of watching many metrics, proposes a cheap first check, and sets a monitoring rule.</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Model answer.</strong> I'd answer &quot;is it a problem?&quot; with &quot;compared with what?&quot; Three questions first: how much do weekly sign-ups normally move, is this week's data complete, and is it an unusual week (a holiday)?</p>\n<p>Suppose the last eight weeks were 10,100, 9,900, 10,200, 10,000, 9,800, 10,100, 10,050 and 9,950. The mean is about 10,010 and the standard deviation about 130, which is about 1.3% of the mean. This week's 9,700 is 3.1% below the mean and about 2.5 standard deviations below it. That is unusual but not overwhelming: with only eight weeks of history my estimate of the normal swing is itself uncertain, and a team that watches dozens of metrics every week will see a 2.5-sigma move by chance from time to time (C12).</p>\n<p>So I would not open a full investigation, and I would not ignore it. A cheap first check, ten minutes: is the data complete, was it a holiday week, and do the segments (platform, channel, country) show one place where it fell? If one segment explains most of the drop, I'd look deeper there. If the drop is spread evenly and there is no cause, I'd set a rule and watch: if next week is also below 9,850 (about the mean minus one and a quarter standard deviations), or if the drop persists for two weeks, I'd investigate fully.</p>\n<p>The decision I'd state: &quot;It is slightly outside the normal range; I'd check segments and the calendar now, and escalate if it persists.&quot; What would change my mind: a concentrated segment drop, or a related metric (activation, revenue) moving too.</p>\n<p><strong>Follow-ups.</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Q.</strong> What if the next two weeks also come in about 3% below the average? <strong>A.</strong> &quot;Then it is no longer noise. One week at 9,700 is about 2.4 standard deviations below the 10,010 mean, unusual but possible; three weeks in a row near 3% below is very unlikely by chance, so it is a trend, and I would open a full diagnosis: segment by channel, country and device, and check for a change in acquisition or tracking. My monitoring rule would flag one week outside two standard deviations for a quick look and three consecutive weeks below one standard deviation for a full investigation.&quot;</li>\n<li><strong>Q.</strong> How do you avoid chasing noise when you watch many metrics? <strong>A.</strong> &quot;By accounting for how many alarms chance produces. With 40 metrics each checked weekly against a two-standard-deviation band, about 4.6% of checks fire by chance, so I would expect nearly two false alerts every week. I would set wider thresholds for metrics that matter less, require a second signal before escalating (for example a funnel metric and a revenue metric both moving), and keep a short list of metrics tied to decisions. The goal is a monitoring process that people trust because it rarely cries wolf.&quot;</li>\n</ul>\n<span id=\"iq-c13-02\"></span>\n<h5>IQ-C13-02 · Beginner · analytics · AN · Google</h5>\n<p><strong>Prompt.</strong> Average watch time per user on a large video platform fell 3% over two weeks. What do you do?</p>\n<p><strong>Rubric.</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Baseline:</strong> &quot;Content must have gotten worse,&quot; or &quot;run an experiment.&quot;</li>\n<li><strong>Strong:</strong> Confirms the change, segments by user type, and checks for a mix shift.</li>\n<li><strong>Standout:</strong> Splits rate and mix with numbers, notes that a growing share of new users lowers an average without any change in behavior, and says what to track instead.</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Model answer.</strong> I know the product only from public information and use. First step zero: the metric is average minutes per user, the window is two weeks, the data is complete and a second metric, sessions, agrees. A 3% move over two weeks is a slide, not a step, which points away from a release or an outage and toward a change in who is using the product or how.</p>\n<p><em>Segment by tenure.</em> An average over users is a mix of new and established viewers. Suppose established users watch 90 minutes in the period and new users 40, and new users were 10% of the base and are now 15% (a marketing push or a launch in a new country).</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Before: 0.90 × 90 + 0.10 × 40 = 81 + 4 = 85 minutes.</li>\n<li>After: 0.85 × 90 + 0.15 × 40 = 76.5 + 6 = 82.5 minutes, which is −2.9%.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>If established users still watch 90 and new users still watch 40, the rate effect is zero and the mix effect is (0.85 − 0.90) × 90 + (0.15 − 0.10) × 40 = −4.5 + 2.0 = −2.5, which is the whole drop. A fall in the average with no change in anyone's behavior.</p>\n<p><em>Confirm.</em> I'd check that established users' watch time is flat, and that the new users' watch time is in line with earlier cohorts at the same age (C05's cohort reading). I'd also check the other mix candidates: device (more phone, less TV), country, and content type.</p>\n<p><em>Decision.</em> If the mix explains it, nothing is broken: more new users is growth, and new users' watch time grows with tenure. I'd change how the metric is reported (by cohort and tenure), and I'd say to stakeholders: &quot;The average fell because new users are a larger share; established users are unchanged.&quot; I'd keep a watch on new-user watch time by age as an input to activation (C05). What would change my mind: if established users' watch time fell, the drop would be a real change in behavior and I'd look at content, recommendations and releases.</p>\n<p><em>Company note.</em> Google is the setting only: <code>Inferred</code> from candidate reports that analytical prompts at Google concern its own products at scale <code>[unverified: candidate-reported]</code>. No Google data or product behavior is claimed.</p>\n<p><strong>Follow-ups.</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Q.</strong> How would you show that the mix of new and established users explains the fall? <strong>A.</strong> &quot;With a split by tenure. Suppose new users are 20% of daily users and watch 20 minutes a day, and established users watch 50. The average is 0.2 × 20 + 0.8 × 50 = 44 minutes. If new users rise to 25% of users with no change in either group's behavior, the average becomes 0.25 × 20 + 0.75 × 50 = 42.5 minutes, a fall of about 3.4% with no change in anyone's habits. If watch time is flat within each tenure group, the fall is a mix effect, and it may even be good news, since it reflects growth.&quot;</li>\n<li><strong>Q.</strong> What would you track instead of the overall average? <strong>A.</strong> &quot;Watch time by tenure cohort (new, one to three months, established), the median and a share-above-threshold such as the share of users watching at least 30 minutes a day, and retention curves by sign-up cohort. These separate behavior change from mix change. I would also report the average with a tenure-adjusted version that holds the user mix fixed, so that leadership can see both what the average is and what the like-for-like change is.&quot;</li>\n</ul>\n<span id=\"iq-c13-03\"></span>\n<h5>IQ-C13-03 · Intermediate · analytics · AN · Apple</h5>\n<p><strong>Prompt.</strong> Daily app downloads on an app store fell 8% week over week (invented). Diagnose.</p>\n<p><strong>Rubric.</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Baseline:</strong> &quot;Maybe fewer people want apps, or there is a competitor.&quot;</li>\n<li><strong>Strong:</strong> Confirms the change, segments by region, device and release, and checks the calendar and store features.</li>\n<li><strong>Standout:</strong> Quantifies contributions, nests cuts to a specific country, builds a ledger with predictions, apportions the change and decides with owners named.</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Model answer.</strong> I know the product only from public information and use. Step zero: downloads per day, averaged over the week, from 1,000,000 to 920,000. I'd check that the week is complete, the definition (does it count re-downloads and updates?) is unchanged, and the normal week-to-week swing, which I'd expect to be a couple of percent. A second source, such as developer-reported installs, should agree.</p>\n<p><em>Locate by region (FW-C13-04).</em> Suppose: North America 300,000 to 295,000; Europe 250,000 to 247,000; Asia-Pacific 350,000 to 278,000; other 100,000 to 100,000. That is −5,000, −3,000, −72,000 and 0, a total of −80,000. Asia-Pacific explains 72,000 of 80,000, 90%.</p>\n<p><em>Nest.</em> Within Asia-Pacific, one country goes from 150,000 to 85,000, down 65,000: 81% of the whole drop (65 of 80), while the other countries in the region fell 7,000.</p>\n<p><em>Ledger (FW-C13-06).</em></p>\n";
